  <header class="qa-header">
    <p class="qa-pillar">Question · Biology</p>
    <h1>What Do Ravens Eat?</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Common Ravens are generalist omnivores — they eat almost anything they can find or overpower: carrion (dead animals), small live prey (rodents, nestlings, rabbits), eggs, invertebrates (insects, earthworms), berries, grain, seeds, and human refuse. Their dietary breadth is one reason they have the widest distribution of any raven species.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Carrion — Their Most Important Food Source</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Carrion is the cornerstone of the raven's diet, particularly in winter and in northern latitudes where prey is harder to catch. Ravens are drawn to large carcasses from considerable distances — there is evidence they can detect carrion by smell as well as by sight, and they learn from watching other ravens and wolves find food.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <p>Ravens have a complex relationship with wolves and other large predators: they follow wolf packs, eat from wolf kills, and may even lead wolves to prey in return for access to the kill. Bernd Heinrich documented this behaviour extensively in New England ravens and wolf–raven associations.<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Live Prey</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens actively hunt small prey including:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Rodents (voles, mice, lemmings) — especially in open tundra and agricultural land.</li>
      <li>Rabbits and leverets — particularly juveniles that cannot escape.</li>
      <li>Nestlings and eggs of other birds — ravens will raid nests and mob nesting colonies.</li>
      <li>Lizards, frogs, and other small vertebrates.</li>
      <li>Large invertebrates — beetles, earthworms, marine invertebrates in coastal areas.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Ravens are capable hunters but prefer prey that is easy to catch; they will not expend energy pursuing difficult prey when easier food is available.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Plant Foods</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens eat berries, fruits, and seeds opportunistically, particularly in late summer and autumn when these are abundant. Grain and cereal crops are taken where ravens live near farmland. In some regions, plant food can make up a significant portion of the summer diet when invertebrates and fruit are plentiful and carrion is scarce.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Human Refuse and Urban Food</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens are highly opportunistic around human settlements. They raid rubbish bins, landfills, campsites, and picnic areas. In some cities (Yellowknife, Canada; Las Vegas, Nevada; parts of Israel and Turkey) raven populations have expanded dramatically due to food availability from human refuse.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup> This is a "science" labelled fact — not a judgement about whether it is desirable.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Food Caching — Storing for Later</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens cache food — they hide surplus food (usually in soil, under vegetation, or in crevices) for later retrieval. They have a strong spatial memory for cache locations and can maintain hundreds of caches simultaneously. They also cache strategically: re-hiding food when they suspect they have been watched by a potential thief.<sup><a href="#src-3">3</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Related Questions</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/questions/how-smart-are-ravens/">How smart are ravens?</a></li>
      <li><a href="/questions/how-long-do-ravens-live/">How long do ravens live?</a></li>
      <li><a href="/compare/raven-vs-crow/">Raven vs crow — what's the difference?</a></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

